\details{
The function supports multiple diseases, geographic levels, year ranges,
and optional stratification. Stratified queries expand results into all
requested combinations (e.g., age by race). When possible, numeric fields
are returned as \code{double} and identifiers as character.

\code{get_atlas()} only downloads. For census tract or block group estimates,
pass county data to \code{\link[=impute_suppressed]{impute_suppressed()}} and then
\code{\link[=extrapolate_from_county]{extrapolate_from_county()}}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{county <- get_atlas("hiv", "county", 2022, stratify_by = "sex")
county <- impute_suppressed(county)
no_county_cases(county)
tracts <- extrapolate_from_county(county)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Supported diseases}

Some values return more than one indicator; the \code{indicator} column says
which row is which.
\itemize{
\item \code{"chlamydia"}
\item \code{"gonorrhea"}
\item \code{"adult syphilis"}: three indicators, one per stage: primary and
secondary syphilis; early non-primary, non-secondary syphilis; and
syphilis of unknown duration or late syphilis.
\item \code{"congenital syphilis"}
\item \code{"tuberculosis"}
\item \code{"hiv"}: two indicators, HIV diagnoses (new diagnoses in the year) and
HIV prevalence (people living with diagnosed HIV).
\item \code{"estimate"}: three modeled HIV indicators: estimated HIV incidence,
estimated HIV prevalence (diagnosed and undiagnosed), and Knowledge of
Status. Knowledge of Status is a percentage, not a count. AtlasPlus says
CDC has postponed release of estimates for HIV incidence and HIV
prevalence, so recent years may be missing; check AtlasPlus for what is
available.
\item \code{"hepatitis"}: three indicators: hepatitis A, acute hepatitis B and acute
hepatitis C.
}

HIV indicators cover people aged 13 and older; the other diseases cover all
ages.

\strong{Geography}
\itemize{
\item \code{"national"}
\item \code{"region"}: the four Census regions (Northeast, Midwest, South, West) and
U.S. territories and freely associated states. Only for
\code{disease = "estimate"}: CDC returns regions only for its HIV estimates.
The \code{fips} column holds CDC's own codes for these, not Census FIPS codes.
\item \code{"state"}
\item \code{"county"}
\item \code{"msa"} (metropolitan statistical area)
}

\strong{Stratification}
The CDC AtlasPlus API supports stratification by one or two dimensions:
\itemize{
\item \code{"age"}
\item \code{"race"}
\item \code{"sex"}
}

Stratification increases the number of returned rows. For example,
\code{stratify_by = c("race", "sex")} returns all race × sex combinations
for each geography-year pair.

This function is a reverse-engineered wrapper around the AtlasPlus
public query system. The CDC does not publish an official API specification,
and internal identifiers may change without notice. If the CDC modifies the
backend schema, requests may fail. Please report issues via GitHub.
